A binary tree is height-balanced if, for every node, the height difference between its left and right subtrees is at most 1.

In simple words:
- The left subtree shouldn't be significantly taller than the right subtree.
- The right subtree shouldn't be significantly taller than the left subtree.
///////////////////////////////
Since the array is already sorted in ascending order, we can pick the middle element as the root.

Why?
- Elements before the middle are smaller → they belong to the left subtree.
- Elements after the middle are greater → they belong to the right subtree.
- This naturally helps us create a height-balanced BST.
********************
We'll work separately on the left and right portions of the middle element, not traverse again from the beginning.
class Solution {

    public TreeNode sortedArrayToBST(int[] nums) {
        return createTree(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);
    }

    public TreeNode createTree(int[] nums, int start, int end) {

        // Base condition
        if (start > end) {
            return null;
        }

        // Find middle element
        int mid = (start + end) / 2;

        // Create root node
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(nums[mid]);

        // Create left subtree
        root.left = createTree(nums, start, mid - 1);

        // Create right subtree
        root.right = createTree(nums, mid + 1, end);

        return root;
    }
}
